   "disease_name": "Acute Intermittent Porphyria",
   "id": "",
   "prognosis": "acute abdominal pain, related to dysfunction of peripheral nervous system, secondary to elevated PBG || Abdominal pain is minimised as evidenced by verbalisation of reduction in pain <2 in the Numerical Rating Scale || Impaired physical mobility related to generalised weakness and wobbling of knees secondary to neurotoxic effect || Normal elimination pattern is maintained as evidenced by regular bowel movement || Effective coping is enhanced as evidenced by following the given instructions and adhering to the treatment and positive attitude towards treatment",
   "treatments": "administration of dextrose and high-carbohydrate diet || Haemin (panhaematin and haem arginate) is the drug of choice || Cimetidine: It is effective for acute porphyric crisis and for long-term management || Pain: It is severe and often requires the use of opiates || Nausea: It can be severe. Nausea and vomiting are controllable with phenothiazine drugs and ondansetron || Tachycardia and hypertension: Preferred medications are beta-blockers, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and calcium channel blockers || Depression: This symptom is common, especially with repeated attacks. Treating the offending symptoms and if needed the judicious use of antidepressants are recommended"
